<h1>Week 3</h1>
<p>
    Hello Again!
</p>
<p>
    I don't always teach classes in the Summer. But I have a few this
    year. What I never really seem to be prepared for is just how fast 
    the 8-week term really is!
</p>
<p>
    I'm working on keeping up with grading your assignments and getting 
    them back to you. Although there has been a delay with having a few
    late assignments and a lot of extra 'beginning-of-the-term' administration.
</p>
<p>
    Reviewing your assignments has been encouraging. I've seen a lot of
    really good work. Remember that all the lab exercises are intended to
    be 'simple.' Simple does not mean easy, but rather, "not complex."
</p>
<p>
    The assignments are intended to exercise a particular objective. Most 
    of the labs will have a guided example in the first exercise. These 
    are expected to be followed as written. Yes, you may want to extend 
    them a bit or experiment a little with them, but overall, they present
    a syntax and design pattern that should correlate to the remaining 
    exercises.
</p>
<p>
    Design patterns are an important part of programming. Starting with 
    a set of requirements, these are usually decomposed until they fit 
    common logic approaches.
</p>
<p>
    Syntax matters. This is true in written, spoken, and programming languages.
    If you violate spoken language rules, you might not be correctly understood.
    If you break programming syntax rules, your code may not execute, or may
    produce incorrect results. This is not unique to Python, although Python
    does have its own peculiarities.
</p>
<p>
    Stay consistent. I've said many times that I write code because I enjoy 
    it. And it's true that the more you do it, the easier it gets, and the more
    rewarding the result.
</p>
<p>
    As a final thought, thank you very much. As a class, you have been doing 
    a great job keeping up to date and submitting your assignments on time. 
    This is a great help to me. If you've missed a couple of deadlines, maybe
    this is a good chance to evaluate if there is a small adjustment you can 
    make to get back and stay on track.
</p>
